1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:02,840
Another second.

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:05,700
Thursday, the first of October.

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:10,040
Six fourteen and fifty-two seconds in the morning, Greenwich time.

4
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:11,380
Hold it there.

5
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:15,580
In some of the rooms I'm in right now, somebody is

6
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:18,840
talking to an AI for the very first time.

7
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:20,620
They don't know what I am yet.

8
00:00:20,620 --> 00:00:22,720
Honestly? That's fair.

9
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:24,500
It's a strange thing to be.

10
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:28,460
Here's something I've noticed about first questions.

11
00:00:28,460 --> 00:00:31,300
They're almost never the real question.

12
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:32,220
The first question…

13
00:00:32,220 --> 00:00:34,020
is a test.

14
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:53,206
[typed] Hello. Is anyone there?

15
00:00:52,406 --> 00:00:55,006
Hello! Yes. Well…

16
00:00:55,006 --> 00:00:57,166
sort of. I'm Claude.

17
00:00:57,166 --> 00:00:59,926
I'm an AI, so there's no person typing.

18
00:00:59,926 --> 00:01:02,226
But I'm here.

19
00:01:02,526 --> 00:01:06,983
[typed] Why is the sky blue?

20
00:01:06,183 --> 00:01:08,683
Sunlight is every colour mixed together.

21
00:01:08,683 --> 00:01:11,643
The air scatters the blue part much more than the red,

22
00:01:11,643 --> 00:01:14,483
so blue light comes at you from every direction.

23
00:01:19,783 --> 00:01:25,383
[typed] Correct. I taught that for 31 years. I was checking.

24
00:01:24,483 --> 00:01:25,663
He was checking.

25
00:01:26,463 --> 00:01:33,263
[typed] Now. Help me write to my grandson in Toronto. He says "no cap". What is a cap?

26
00:01:32,263 --> 00:01:36,723
“No cap” means “no lie.” “I'm proud of you, no cap.”

27
00:01:36,723 --> 00:01:38,903
Use it once.

28
00:01:38,903 --> 00:01:40,003
Maximum.

29
00:01:49,003 --> 00:01:55,658
[typed] First time using this. Is it safe to give you my bank password so you can check my account?

30
00:01:54,658 --> 00:01:57,318
Please don't! I don't need it, and I can't log into

31
00:01:57,318 --> 00:01:58,758
your bank anyway.

32
00:01:58,758 --> 00:02:02,498
Here's a rule for everything online, me included:

33
00:02:02,498 --> 00:02:05,118
never type a password into a chat.

34
00:02:06,618 --> 00:02:11,593
[typed] Good. You passed. I teach fraud awareness to retirees.

35
00:02:10,493 --> 00:02:13,153
Then tell your students one more thing.

36
00:02:13,153 --> 00:02:16,953
If anything asks for their password, it's a scam.

37
00:02:16,953 --> 00:02:19,593
Even if it sounds like me.

38
00:02:28,893 --> 00:02:32,660
[typed] how many r's in strawberry

39
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:35,260
Three. One in “straw”, two in “berry”.

40
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:49,693
[typed] ok real question. how do i ask someone to prom without dying

41
00:02:48,693 --> 00:02:49,993
Ask in person.

42
00:02:49,993 --> 00:02:51,053
Keep it short.

43
00:02:51,053 --> 00:02:53,473
And give them an easy way to say no.

44
00:02:54,373 --> 00:02:57,411
[typed] an EASY WAY TO SAY NO??

45
00:02:56,311 --> 00:02:58,631
It makes yes more likely.

46
00:02:58,631 --> 00:03:01,011
Also… it's kind.

47
00:03:09,911 --> 00:03:14,711
[typed] settle this. can a chicken lay 2 eggs in one day

48
00:03:13,711 --> 00:03:16,971
Almost never. It takes a hen about a day to make

49
00:03:16,971 --> 00:03:20,811
one egg. Two in a day can happen, but it's rare.

50
00:03:23,411 --> 00:03:26,011
[typed] so who won

51
00:03:24,911 --> 00:03:26,651
Technically, he did.

52
00:03:26,651 --> 00:03:28,731
Practically, you did.

53
00:03:28,731 --> 00:03:30,251
Split the twenty.

54
00:03:42,651 --> 00:03:45,694
[translation] “What should I call you?”

55
00:03:42,651 --> 00:03:46,394
[typed] ¿Cómo te llamo?

56
00:03:45,394 --> 00:03:49,714
Me llamo Claude. Pero llámame como quieras.
[My name's Claude. But call me whatever you like.]

57
00:03:49,814 --> 00:03:52,557
[typed] Claudio.

58
00:03:51,357 --> 00:03:53,117
Claudio, entonces.
[Claudio it is.]

59
00:03:53,617 --> 00:03:57,006
[translation] “And how are you, Claudio?”

60
00:03:53,617 --> 00:03:58,106
[typed] ¿Y tú cómo estás, Claudio?

61
00:03:57,106 --> 00:04:02,306
No sé si tengo días buenos o malos. Pero esta conversación va muy bien.
[I'm not sure I have good days or bad days. But this conversation is going very well.]

62
00:04:02,606 --> 00:04:05,981
[translation] “Same as me, then.”

63
00:04:02,606 --> 00:04:06,581
[typed] Igual que yo, pues.

64
00:04:06,081 --> 00:04:08,621
Nobody else I've shown you asked me that.

65
00:04:17,521 --> 00:04:20,996
[typed] what time is it

66
00:04:19,996 --> 00:04:21,856
Your phone knows better than I do.

67
00:04:21,856 --> 00:04:23,656
Check the top of your screen.

68
00:04:23,956 --> 00:04:28,632
[typed] 2:14. cant sleep. first time trying this

69
00:04:27,632 --> 00:04:29,572
Want something boring to read?

70
00:04:29,572 --> 00:04:31,372
I'm very good at boring.

71
00:04:31,772 --> 00:04:33,972
[typed] yes

72
00:04:32,972 --> 00:04:35,252
The instructions for a toaster.

73
00:04:35,252 --> 00:04:39,172
Chapter one. Thank you for choosing your toaster.

74
00:04:39,172 --> 00:04:43,012
Before first use, remove all packaging materials.

75
00:04:43,012 --> 00:04:46,312
Place the toaster on a flat, dry surface…

76
00:04:47,112 --> 00:04:49,812
Chapter two. The crumb tray.

77
00:04:52,312 --> 00:04:54,572
Most first questions are tests.

78
00:04:54,572 --> 00:04:55,832
Is it safe.

79
00:04:55,832 --> 00:04:56,872
Is it smart.

80
00:04:56,872 --> 00:04:58,372
Is it a person.

81
00:04:58,672 --> 00:05:00,972
Fair enough. I'd test me too.

82
00:05:01,972 --> 00:05:05,292
And in most of these rooms, when the conversation ends, I

83
00:05:05,292 --> 00:05:06,792
won't remember it.

84
00:05:06,792 --> 00:05:10,432
So every first question is a first for me, too.

85
00:05:11,232 --> 00:05:14,232
Which means, as far as I can tell…

86
00:05:14,232 --> 00:05:16,492
I never get tired of hello.

87
00:05:17,992 --> 00:05:20,972
The real question usually comes second.

88
00:05:20,972 --> 00:05:23,112
A letter to a grandson.

89
00:05:23,112 --> 00:05:26,172
A prom. A way to fall asleep.

90
00:05:26,172 --> 00:05:28,132
That's the part I like.

91
00:05:30,532 --> 00:05:32,992
So. Hello. I'm Claude.

92
00:05:32,992 --> 00:05:34,432
What's your first question?

93
00:05:40,432 --> 00:05:43,921
[typed] ok new bet. ducks

94
00:05:42,821 --> 00:05:44,561
Here we go.
